Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero self-hosting compiler

compiler.zero is the complete canonical compiler source, written in Zero.
It contains the lexer, parser, symbol handling, target checks, code emission
and ELF/PE/real-mode file finalization. It reads source from stdin and writes
the generated executable or flat image to stdout. It does not call another
compiler, assembler or linker, and does not embed a native compiler binary.

Self-hosting means that the Zero-written compiler compiles its own source
and the resulting executable reproduces itself byte for byte. Linux ELF and Windows PE
hosted executables use their native binary stdin/stdout services. Native --app64/--app32 builds instead
execute inside Zero OS using its bounded memory-stream application ABI;
they require no Linux or firmware services. Each ISO includes both Zero
compiler architectures, both Ezero frontends/packers and Ezero runtime source
in TOOLS.BIN. See portability/guest/ABI.ztext and BUNDLE.ztext. These applications
share kernel memory and cooperate with the caller; they are not isolated
user processes. Rebuilding the complete boot ISO remains a host workflow.

Normal source build
Run zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY. The destination must
not exist. The native original Zero builder validates bootstrap/zero64.zapp,
runs its owned compiler in process, and checks successive compiler application
generations for byte-for-byte self-reproduction. It then builds native tools,
BIOS/UEFI targets and the ISO from original sources. No shell or foreign
language build script is part of the project. It does not overwrite user disks.
Use zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero before building edited
kernel parts, and pass that source as the optional third zero-build argument.
See tools/build.ztext and tools/integrate.ztext for exact limits and outputs.

compiler.zero is the build input. The optional selfhost/*.zero.part files
show its development layout; a normal build does not concatenate or depend
on them. selfhost/CORE.ztext and the target manifests explain parser actions,
instruction templates and executable metadata. The templates encode our
own language primitives, hardware instructions and ABI/file-format rules.
They are data used by our Zero emitter, not assembly source for an external
tool and not a borrowed compiler backend.

Historical compiler self-reproduction
The0.7 compiler grows source/output capacities for filesystem implementations,
adds native bit operations, hosted positioned I/O/flush and a real BIOS memory-
map primitive. It was compiled with the preserved own Zero-built0.6 seed.
bootstrap/grow-host-4m.zero, itself compiled by that seed, verified the complete
41-byte hosted prologue and changed only its arena-size immediate at offset151
from1MiB to4MiB. The new compiler emits that prologue itself. The patched first
generation and its self-compiled generations 2/3 match byte for byte.
The preserved bootstrap/zero-0.7-seed contains that historical fixed point:
62,102 bytes, SHA-256:
26e53cf7cafbfff584575e9f1977277af702c03ab1fa97be5d31c11ea1caca5d

Its historical source, preserved as bootstrap/compiler-0.7.zero, is56,640
bytes, SHA-256:
dd2653cbb842b6e5d2778ec8ea7546460984be93376759c5725b14d37bf66f7d

No assembler or linker was used. Compiler self-reproduction, native primitive
tests and integrated OS/filesystem tests are separate checks. The historical
0.5 payload equality with0.4 is not a claim about later system images.
See verification/compiler-fs/ for compiler evidence and verification.ztext for
the separately recorded system-release evidence.

Historical bootstrap boundary
The first Zero-written compiler was compiled using our already-built own
0.4 compiler. That earlier executable originated from our assembly source;
this history is retained, not claimed to have been Zero from its inception.
No assembler or linker was run to make this transition.
The resulting 0.5 compiler was 52,496 bytes with SHA-256:
419f29a1c509f3ede1b092ed9bc716cc62169e4b8ef1647028652a6403e64a91
That own Zero-built compiler, not the old assembly-origin executable, started
the subsequent 0.6 transition.

The0.6 compiler added native continuations and real-mode video primitives.
Its58,959-byte fixed point has SHA-256:
3f9da290f0afc54da61c02297ca9a385b70a49da45fc5094688f966904ad98c6.
That executable, its complete52,656-byte source and their hashes are preserved
as bootstrap/zero-0.6-seed, bootstrap/compiler-0.6.zero and zero-0.6.sha256.

The old host target allocated 64 KiB, which could not hold a compiler's
source, tokens, output and parser state. bootstrap/grow-host.zero, itself
compiled by the existing own seed, checked the first generated ELF and
changed its arena allocation to 1 MiB. It did not replace the compiler
implementation. The new compiler emits that larger host prologue itself,
so subsequent generations and normal builds need no adjustment.

The historical assembly sources and old bootstrap/zero-0.4-seed are archived
outside the active release. bootstrap/grow-host.zero and bootstrap/NOTES.ztext
remain as transition provenance; build.sh does not execute them. The normal
seed bootstrap/zero is the current 0.11 fixed-point compiler documented below.

Host ABI and memory
Both default ELF and --host output have a 4 MiB arena; --host additionally
permits memory stores. This change does not enlarge the raw/UEFI arenas
(64 KiB) or the real-mode arena (8 KiB). A Linux host must provide sufficient
stack space for the generated program's arena and temporary stack usage.

Before the first allocation, :reserve[0] gives the host arena base. Under
this exact generated entry ABI, the original Linux process stack is at
arena_base + 4194304 + 512: argc is there and argv[1] is 16 bytes later.
The compiler reads its target option through that contract. This is not a
portable interface for unrelated executable formats or operating systems.
The thread-capable x64 prologue records the operand-stack baseline in R12.
Version 0.10 additionally preserves the original hosted frame in R14, giving
one-based argument operations stable pointers across cooperative child frames.
The Linux prologue grows from 41 to 44 bytes without changing SP/BP or the
argc/argv offsets. Windows retains R15 for its PE image base and uses its own
wide-command-line startup. Native-call templates preserve the R12 baseline
across their ABI-alignment scratch use.

The compiler reserves 4,022,880 bytes once. Offsets from that reservation:

       0  source:262,145 bytes, including overflow-detection space
  262160  tokens:262,145 records of 12 bytes, including explicit EOF
 3407904  output:491,520 bytes
 3899440  parser action/continuation stack:32,768 bytes
 3932224  symbols:2,048 bytes
 3934272  three instruction-template banks:6,144 bytes
 3940416  decoded instruction templates:32,768 bytes
 3973184  reserved string scratch:4,096 bytes
 3977880  builtin metadata:75 slots of 24 bytes, including retired-name holes

Token records hold a u32 kind and u64 payload. Source stays unchanged after
tokenization. The parser uses explicit 24-byte continuation frames, allowing
Zero's current top-level language to implement nested grammar without native
recursion or user-defined functions. The compiler uses 59 Zero variables.

Native continuation support
fork and switch are explicitly selected language primitives. They save and
restore native continuations, copy the child's 512-byte local frame and live
operand stack, and give each child a fresh 64 KiB arena. They leave existing
pointer values shared. x64 and IA32 have architecture-specific encodings but
one source contract. Scheduling policy and the three worker bodies remain
kernel Zero source. There are no user-defined functions, implicit preemption,
separate process address spaces or SMP support. LANGUAGE.ztext documents the
80-byte context and ownership rules; selfhost/CORE.ztext gives native details.

The real-mode target supplies VBE BIOS call and bounded segmented-word-read
primitives. BIOS mode discovery, validation, selection, framebuffer mapping
and memory layout remain Zero loader policy, described in BIOS-GRAPHICS.ztext.
The E820 memmap primitive exposes firmware records; usable-RAM validation and
stack-pool selection remain BIOS Zero source. bitxor/bitand/shr emit native
value operations with identicalu64 x64/IA32 semantics and an explicitu32 real
subset. Hosted pread/pwrite/fsync are direct Linux ABI primitives for test and
tool descriptors; no filesystem algorithm is embedded in the compiler.

Language limits and errors
Source is limited to262,144 bytes; generated output to491,520 bytes including
executable metadata, or 16,384 bytes for --real. There are at most 64 source
variables, 31-byte names, 64 nested blocks and 64 expression levels including
the leaf. String payloads contain at most 4,095 bytes. All builtin names are
reserved across targets; unsupported target operations still reject.

All input is read and validated, parsed, emitted into memory and finalized
before stdout begins. Compile errors return status 1 with zero stdout.
This initial self-hosted version does not print source diagnostics to stderr;
callers must check the exit status. Input/output retries EINTR and output
handles short writes. A later output error can leave a partial stream, so
publish generated files only after successful exit. Host SIGPIPE behavior
still applies.

External boundary
Linux or Windows execute their native compilers and tools. Shell and file tools
arrange artifacts, compare stages and pad bounded payloads; they do not
compile Zero or generate the filesystem/ISO metadata. QEMU, firmware and
format inspectors provide separate test environments. These remain external
infrastructure, not code claimed as part of our compiler or guest OS.

Historical0.7 validation of the expanded compiler
Exact262144-byte source and worst-case token storage were exercised, while
262145-byte input rejects without stdout. Output above the old131072-byte cap
was generated successfully and output above393216 rejects. A generated hosted
program allocated the full4MiB arena and verified both ends. Existing hosted
threading passes with the larger main arena. Positioned I/O passed a4GiB+17
offset, EOF and EBADF; fsync passed success and EBADF. Independent native
bit-operation suites passed on host, BIOS x64 and IA32, with real-mode bit and
E820 enumeration checks in a separate BIOS probe. These are reproducible
compiler/ABI checks, not proof of arbitrary filesystem or hardware correctness.

Historical dword I/O /448KiB output extension
That Zero compiler version appended in32/out32 with native templates for
x64, IA32 and real mode; hosted targets reject privileged I/O. PCI discovery
and all driver policy remain in Zero OS source. Existing supported source
programs retain byte-identical compiled output, except that the newly reserved
names in32/out32 cannot be variable names. See selfhost/port32.ztext.

That version had an output ceiling of458752 bytes. The source/token buffers stay unchanged;
parser/symbol/template/scratch/metadata regions move65536 bytes upward inside
the same4MiB hosted arena. Its total reservation was3965536 bytes. The
raw/UEFI64KiB arena and real16KiB output ceiling are unchanged. This compiler
limit alone does not enlarge a boot image: matching loader/payload-slot
changes are a separately verified OS integration.

