Current 0.11 native host interoperability
The canonical source is 88763 bytes, SHA256:
a76aeeda865a3fbbc887c3d9a66c8a02bd1ff5e46aacd1d359a361033c170b7a
bootstrap/zero and build/zero are 93086 bytes, SHA256:
d89762ea95d3591d95e7f9a66e1015d8124bf87f702dac65ce25547f2ec4ed3f
The preserved 0.10 source/seed are bootstrap/compiler-0.10.zero and
bootstrap/zero-0.10-seed, with their manifest bootstrap/zero-0.10.ztext.
That own seed compiled the new source directly. All three successive native
generations are byte-identical. Normal builds compare generations1/2 from the
new fixed point. The earlier0.9 source/seed and transition remain preserved.

Eighteen native host operations add host identity, one-based user arguments,
absolute program path, bounded file operations, file-flush requests, bounded
native-thread waits and explicit RW/RX memory ownership. Direct Linux syscalls
and Windows KERNEL32 services implement the
platform boundary; no foreign compiler/backend/runtime is embedded. Windows
startup converts its wide command line to UTF-8 and uses our original bounded
argument parser. It supports 63 user arguments and 4095 total converted bytes.
Guest targets compile the shared operations but return explicit unavailable
sentinels. Existing hardware target programs retain their instruction output.
See LANGUAGE.ztext and portability/host/ABI.ztext for semantics and target gates.

Native zero/ezero launchers are original Zero programs. They load the original
application images into owned memory, change code pages from RW to RX, and
call the guest ABI. No shell/Python/.cmd launcher is needed to compile a user
program. Full ISO construction, tests and packaging remain host orchestration.
The guest compiler images are 94438 bytes x64 and 124286 bytes IA32, each within
its 131072-byte bundle slot. The current Ezero runtime body uses a 65536-byte
slot; the complete TOOLS.BIN remains 1 MiB. BUNDLE.ztext defines the exact layout.
Guest compiler SHA256 values:
x64 cad49cb2f5ee5a7bf09d8b221949ece97d23d07231d4c0e9e7a62a1eb30dec96
IA32 74de4b0eea075c451752289be79c65229a4e8962f37e50b0cc57b667c25ad852

Verification passed136 focused durability/wait checks and433 existing common
parser/target/fallback checks, with native x64 guest fallback execution and
exact hardware-target output parity. All17 Linux host-ABI behavior cases pass.
The new Linux tests observe timespec arguments and injectEINTR/EIO/EFAULT to
exercise retries and failure reporting. They do not simulate real power loss.
Windows tests cover21 new flush/wait assertions, native PE self-reproduction
and existing API/argument/file behavior under Wine10. The new FlushFileBuffers
and Sleep imports retain the existing RVAs, with25 entries plus a null thunk.
These are not a native Microsoft Windows machine test, and real
Windows reparse-point rejection remains dynamically unverified because the
Wine creation fixture is a stub. Current reports are in
portability/host/durability; historical0.10 reports remain under
verification/host-api/ and portability/host/*verification.json.
File flush requests do not establish directory/rename durability or physical
power-loss guarantees. Final OS-image validation is separate.
